=== 1970 to present ===
British Columbia introduced an ABC-123 serial format on its passenger plates in 1970. A split-alphabet system was used, with each letter advancing from A through K, or from L through X (the letters I, O, Q, U, Y and Z were omitted). Serials were thus issued in the following eight series: AAA–KKJ, AAL–KKX, ALA–KXK, ALL–KXX, LAA–XKK, LAL–XKX, LLA–XXK and LLL–XXX.<ref name="allaboutlicenseplatesBC"/> When the ABC-123 format was exhausted in 2001, a reversed format, 123-ABC, was introduced, with the same split-alphabet system and thus the same eight series. Upon the exhaustion of this format in 2014, an AB1-23C format was introduced, with the split-alphabet system discontinued and each letter thus advancing from A through X. In 2025, another format, A12-3BC was introduced, with a new alphabet system which allows the letters U, Y, and Z to be used (with the letters I, O, and Q still omitted).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dicdiquin |first=Christopher |date=June 9, 2025 |title=Bulletin 33 - Changes to B.C. license plates |url=https://partners.icbc.com/assets/5GMseymkqs8E4Otb8osoGQ/bulletin-33-new-licence-plate-configurations.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250626034355/https://partners.icbc.com/assets/5GMseymkqs8E4Otb8osoGQ/bulletin-33-new-licence-plate-configurations.pdf |archive-date=June 26, 2025 |website=partners.icbc.com}}</ref>

